Email. that is, folks. And here's an answer to one of your emails.

161
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:37,000
Hey, Bill Chapman is the Scout Master of Troop 736 in Rancho Santa Margarita, California.

162
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:42,000
He said we seem to have a perennial problem with rowdy troop meetings.

163
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:46,000
It's kind of like it was the best of times and the worst of times scenario.

164
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:50,000
For example, at a recent troop meeting there was no pre-opening plan.

165
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:55,000
The Scouts did their opening as usual and skill instruction was really just the senior patrol leader

166
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:58,000
talking to Scouts about how to succeed a camp for about 15 minutes.

167
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:04,000
It would have taken him five minutes to do, but he had to continually stop and wait while everybody quit talking.

168
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:08,000
The patrol meetings that evening were actually focused on one really rowdy patrol

169
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:13,000
and our other two patrols just kind of floundered because there wasn't any plan for anything.

170
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:17,000
Right after the troop meeting, the patrol leaders council met for about a half hour

171
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:21,000
and they enthusiastically addressed problems like rowdiness,

172
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:26,000
which is a frequent topic of discussion at our patrol leaders council meetings.

173
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:29,000
At times I thought the whole evening was a total disaster.

174
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:31,000
The Scouts are all talking and yelling at once.

175
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:34,000
There's no respect given to the senior patrol leader.

176
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:37,000
I wanted to jump in and say stop the noise and chaos,

177
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:40,000
but I knew that that might just make things worse.

178
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:45,000
And after that evening a committee member said that they have actually visited other troops

179
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:50,000
that are really well behaved and said something like: you know, we need to learn how to do that.

180
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:53,000
As I drove home I thought about it and I have to question whether or not

181
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:57,000
the troops that are really well behaved are actually youth led or not.

182
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:03,000
I know adults think that Scouts ought to sit and behave and listen attentively,

183
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:08,000
but I don't know if that's the way boys naturally behave if they're running things.

184
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:11,000
Can you train youth leaders to get the troop to be revered and respectful,

185
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:13,000
or is that just not in the carts?

186
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:17,000
Does it come with practice in patients, or am I just wishing for something that I don't want?

187
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:22,000
Well, Bill, you know this is one of the things that I could talk about on every podcast,

188
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:24,000
and in one way or another, I guess that I do,

189
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:28,000
but a lot of this is a trick of perspective.

190
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:33,000
I think we need to remember that Scouts simply do not see and experience things as adults

191
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:35,000
because they're not adults.

192
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:37,000
And I know that sounds very simple and pedantic

193
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:40,000
and I don't mean it to sound snide or cynical,

194
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:44,000
but you know, that's the one thing that I have to remind myself of all the time.

195
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:48,000
My Scouts don't experience the world as adults because they're not

196
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:49,000
Pretty simple, huh.

197
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:53,000
So, Bill, there's a couple of things that tell me maybe this doesn't need to be fixed.

198
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:56,000
and to get an idea whether or not it does need to be fixed,

199
00:09:56,000 --> 00:10:00,000
talk to your youth leadership and a few younger Scouts, you know,

200
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:04,000
and give them some big, open-ended questions like: how did the meeting go?

201
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:05,000
What do you think of tonight's meeting?

202
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:07,000
How did things go for you?

203
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:12,000
And just see what they have to say, because if they have something that's bothering them,

204
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:15,000
they're going to tell you and then you have something to work with.

205
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:19,000
But the standard response usually is: yeah, it was fun, you know, and that's about it.

206
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:21,000
From a Scout that's a thumbs up right,

207
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:25,000
And they might not be able to explain why it was fun, but they had a good time.

208
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:29,000
Maybe they learned a few things, but they got to do the work of Scouting

209
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:32,000
because they were there with their patrol, with their troop.

210
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:33,000
So ask those questions.

211
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:35,000
Don't try and tell them anything.

212
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:37,000
Don't tell them the way you feel or what you saw.

213
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:39,000
Ask them what they experienced.

214
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:42,000
Then you'll have an idea if there's anything really to fix.

215
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:45,000
But, like I said, there's a couple of things that indicate to me

216
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:48,000
that there's nothing necessarily that you need to fix here.

217
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:50,000
You had a bunch of Scouts at a meeting-

218
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:52,000
That indicates that they want to be there,

219
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:54,000
And you had your patrol leaders council get together

220
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:58,000
and they spent a half hour enthusiastically- your word- discussing things.

221
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:00,000
That sounds like a big win to me,

222
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:03,000
And I'm not saying we shouldn't have high expectations of our Scouts,

223
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:07,000
but what you explained to me, I think, is to be expected.

224
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:09,000
It's a little chaotic, It's very loud.

225
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:12,000
Scouts are not necessarily very respectful of their peers

226
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:14,000
when their peers are standing up and talking

227
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:16,000
And when they're in a big herd like that.

228
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:20,000
they're almost never going to become highly organized and efficient

229
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:23,000
at a troop level or in the big herd like that,

230
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:25,000
because the troop isn't really all that important.

231
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:28,000
Everything important happens in the patrol.

232
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:32,000
Patrols do get things done and they are loud and messy at doing them.

233
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:34,000
Sometimes it doesn't look like much.

234
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:37,000
You do have to look hard at what goes on to see what is happening.

235
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:40,000
But if you train your eye and you train your perspective

236
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:44,000
you get pretty good at seeing the positive things that are going on.

237
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:46,000
And you know you're right.

238
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:50,000
Ideally, from an adult perspective, we would like to see the Scouts

239
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:52,000
sitting with their hands folded neatly in their laps,

240
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:55,000
respectfully listening to the senior patrol leader,

241
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:59,000
like a garden club listening to someone telling them how to prune their roses.

242
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:03,000
But that would be very strange because you know for the most part

243
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:07,000
people the age of our Scouts. they're boisterous and rowdy

244
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:09,000
and they want to talk at the same time

245
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:11,000
And you know they're going to figure this out

246
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:13,000
and the senior patrol leader is going to get the message across.

247
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:16,000
but it's not going to be nice and neat like a garden club meeting.

248
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:19,000
And the other thing is we can't see what they're like when we aren't watching,

249
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:21,000
because there's an observer effect.

250
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:24,000
Your Scouts are just like you when you were a kid

251
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:27,000
and that can be a comforting or terrifying thought

252
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:30,000
depending on how you- what you were like as a kid.

253
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:32,000
So let's face it, we were all kids once.

254
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:34,000
It was a long time ago. but you know what?

255
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:37,000
Basically, things haven't changed all that much.

256
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:40,000
We knew how to appear, to pay attention, to be polite and respectful,

257
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:42,000
even when we weren't,

258
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:44,000
When we were just kind of hanging out with our friends.

259
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:48,000
we did not act like adults because we weren't adults,

260
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:49,000
We were just kids.

261
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:52,000
Our Scouts spend a huge percentage of their lives

262
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:54,000
sitting and paying attention respectfully.

263
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:58,000
They're capable of doing it and they get to practice doing that

264
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:01,000
for hours and hours every day at school.

265
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:04,000
It would be a shame if they didn't have an opportunity,

266
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:06,000
like Scouting, to do something a little bit different,

267
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:09,000
To get into the maelstrom and the chaos

268
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:14,000
and the noise of doing an activity

269
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:17,000
that isn't administered by adults but is led by their peers

270
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:20,000
And that's always going to be exciting and engaging

271
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:21,000
and fun for Scouts.

272
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:23,000
It's going to look like chaos to us,

273
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:27,000
But the thing to do is to begin to train your eye

274
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:31,000
and adopt a perspective where you can see Scouting at work

275
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:33,000
even in the midst of all that.

276
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:35,000
I got this question this past week

277
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:37,000
and, of course, due to the nature of the question

278
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:40,000
it's, the center will remain anonymous.

279
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:43,000
We discovered recently that we had made a mistake,

280
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:46,000
crediting a Scout with a position of responsibility

281
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:49,000
that didn't actually qualify for the rank.

282
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:52,000
Somehow we missed this, both at the Scout Master Conference

283
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:54,000
and the Board of Review, and went on

284
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:56,000
to award the rank to the Scout.

285
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:58,000
We discovered the mistake after that.

286
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:00,000
Now what do we do?

287
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:03,000
Before I tell you what to do or what not to do,

288
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:05,000
let me tell you something that I know

289
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:07,000
almost for a dead certainty. okay,

290
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:10,000
Of all the people listening to this podcast,

291
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:12,000
when they heard that you made a mistake

292
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:14,000
by crediting a Scout in a position of responsibility

293
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:16,000
that didn't actually qualify for the rank,

294
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:18,000
they nodded their heads and they said,

295
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:20,000
yep, been there before.

296
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:23,000
They probably have made a similar type of mistake.

297
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:25,000
So, most important thing,

298
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:27,000
you made a mistake. you're not alone.

299
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:29,000
This happens from time to time.

300
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,000
So you discover you've made a mistake

301
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:33,000
in advancing a Scout like this,

302
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:35,000
and now what do you do?

303
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:37,000
Well, the golden rule here,

304
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:40,000
according to me, is avoiding penalizing Scouts

305
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:42,000
for the mistakes of adults.

306
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:44,000
There was nothing about what happened

307
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:46,000
that was within the Scout's control,

308
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:49,000
so the Scout should not be affected

309
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:51,000
by the mistake that an adult made.

310
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:53,000
And what do you do next?

311
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:55,000
Well, I don't know that. I would necessarily

312
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:57,000
even tell the Scout a mistake was made

313
00:14:57,000 --> 00:14:59,000
and I certainly wouldn't penalize him

314
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:01,000
for the mistake that I made in any way, shape or form.

315
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:03,000
This is a different story

316
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:06,000
if somebody was willfully or knowingly

317
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:08,000
trying to skirt a requirement.

318
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:10,000
but that's not the case here.

319
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:12,000
It was a simple error.

320
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:14,000
But this is a really good opportunity

321
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:16,000
to sit down with the adults involved

322
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:18,000
and say: look, you know what,

323
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:20,000
make sure that that doesn't happen again.

324
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:22,000
And that's the way I would handle it.

325
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:24,000
I'm sure there would be differing opinions

326
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:26,000
as to why I would do that,

327
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:28,000
but the concept of not penalizing Scouts

328
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:30,000
for the mistakes adults make-

329
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:32,000
I think if you go to the Guide to Advancement

330
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:34,000
and you read ready,

331
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:37,000
Here we go- 7.0.4.7,,

332
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:39,000
which is entitled

333
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:41,000
The Limited Recourse for Unearned Merit Badges,

334
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:44,000
You get the spirit of what I'm saying.

335
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:47,000
Let's not penalize Scouts for our mistakes.

336
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:50,000
As with anything, policy, procedure, advancement wise-

337
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:52,000
what you're hearing is my best advice,

338
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:54,000
based on my experience and my understanding.

339
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:56,000
Go to the resources, talk to people,

340
00:15:56,000 --> 00:15:58,000
make sure that you get it right.

341
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:01,000
Don't just take my word for anything.

342
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:03,000
Get it sorted out for yourself too.

343
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:05,000
And I did hear back from the folks

344
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:07,000
that sent me the email and they said

345
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:09,000
thanks for the advice.

346
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:11,000
We were able to work with our advancement committee

347
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:13,000
and we agreed that the Scouts shouldn't be penalized.

348
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:15,000
So we're moving forward, and that was great to hear.

349
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:17,000
So I have one other email this week

350
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:20,000
from Danny, who's the Scoutmaster Troop 41,

351
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:21,000
and he wrote in to say

352
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:23,000
what happens to Scouts who quote

353
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:24,000
eagle out, unquote,

354
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:27,000
the ones who can't wait to get their eagle and run.

355
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:30,000
If a Scoutmaster knows this is about to happen,

356
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:31,000
what can he do?

357
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:33,000
I mean, we do want the rank of eagle

358
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:35,000
to mean something besides its time to go,

359
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:38,000
And it means a lot to us as adults.

360
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:40,000
so why don't Scouts seem to get it?

361
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:42,000
We just had the first guy advance to Eagle

362
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:44,000
since I've been Scoutmaster

363
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:46,000
And I don't really know if there's a question in here

364
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:47,000
that can be answered,

365
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:50,000
but it's been on my mind for a few weeks.

366
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:52,000
Well, Dan, I understand the feeling,

367
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:57,000
because we all want the rank of eagle to mean something,

368
00:16:57,000 --> 00:17:00,000
And I think that causes us to get a little overheated

369
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:02,000
sometimes and talk about:

370
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:04,000
oh, this is really, really meaningful

371
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:06,000
and you really need to pay attention to it

372
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:08,000
and you really need to understand what's going on.

373
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:11,000
And we really want Eagle Scouts to act certain ways

374
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:13,000
and hold certain attitudes.

375
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:15,000
We really want to hand them that badge

376
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:18,000
and have a total transformation happen in their lives,

377
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:22,000
because we want the rank of eagle to mean something.

378
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:24,000
And I know exactly how you feel, Dan,

379
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:26,000
because I got befuzzled by this for a long time.

380
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:29,000
I wanted to see something more from my Eagle Scouts.

381
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:32,000
Basically, I really wanted them to act like adults

382
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:35,000
and to understand what I felt and what I understood.

383
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:37,000
But I got over it

384
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:39,000
because I came to understand two basic things.

385
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:42,000
There's only one standard for becoming an Eagle Scout

386
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:44,000
and that's the requirements.

387
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:46,000
Getting the requirements for Eagle done is challenging,

388
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:48,000
but it's fairly simple and straightforward.

389
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:50,000
There is no continuing qualification

390
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:53,000
for being an Eagle Scout once you've completed the requirements.

391
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:56,000
And the second thing that helped me get over this

392
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:59,000
was understanding that anybody who becomes an Eagle Scout

393
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:01,000
once they get the badge,

394
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:05,000
they spend the rest of their lives becoming an Eagle Scout-

395
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:09,000
Our Scouts who earn Eagle and their teenagers.

396
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:13,000
they haven't really finished anything but a set of requirements.

397
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:15,000
They're really just beginning.

398
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:17,000
The important thing to realize

399
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:20,000
is that once a Scout earns Eagle rank,

400
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:24,000
he begins the lifelong process of becoming an Eagle Scout.

401
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:27,000
Rather than trying to make them understand something

402
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:30,000
or projecting obligations on them,

403
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:33,000
ask them about what they already understand.

404
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:36,000
See what they have been thinking about,

405
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:38,000
And it may surprise you.

406
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:42,000
I learned that I would always be disappointed in Eagle Scouts

407
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:45,000
if I think that the award comes with a bunch of strings attached,

408
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:47,000
because it doesn't, and that's a good thing.

409
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:50,000
Eagle Scouts get to live their own lives.

410
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:54,000
Very few Scout-age boys think like mature adults,

411
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:56,000
because they aren't.

412
00:18:56,000 --> 00:19:00,000
They have, hopefully, a long life ahead of them

413
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:04,000
to figure things out, just like you had and just like I had.

414
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:06,000
If we've done our work well as Scouters,

415
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:08,000
if they did, to become an Eagle Scout,

416
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:11,000
we'll inspire them to go on and become good people.

417
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:14,000
So I'm going to get up on my soapbox for a moment, please.

418
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:17,000
And, Danny, this isn't intended for you,

419
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:19,000
This is for everybody, and I don't think you're an old groany

420
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:21,000
complaining guy, but you brought this subject up,

421
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:23,000
so I want to preach for a moment

422
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:26,000
Now listen, this whole thing about quote

423
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:28,000
eagling out. unquote

424
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:33,000
is just another one of these stupid little phrases

425
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:36,000
that somebody started using ages ago

426
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:38,000
and has perpetuated itself

427
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:41,000
and gives old people like me

428
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:43,000
something to sit and grumble about

429
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:46,000
while we're at a picnic table at summer camp.

430
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,000
Well, you know, they just eagle out

431
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:51,000
These boys just anymore. they get the eagle badge

432
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:54,000
and then they leave and blah, blah, blah, blah.

433
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,000
Look, I've been around Scouting now

434
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
for about 35 years as an adult.

435
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:00,000
35 years ago people were saying that.

436
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,000
60 years ago people were saying that

437
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:04,000
Let's get over ourselves here,

438
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:07,000
So let's stop using stupid terms like that

439
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:10,000
and let's talk to our Scouts on an individual basis

440
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:12,000
and find out what they understand.

441
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:14,000
You're going to be surprised by what they understand.

442
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:15,000
They understand a lot.

443
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:17,000
And if you sit around and complain

444
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:18,000
and grouse about your Scouts,

445
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:19,000
well they're not going to talk to you,

446
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:22,000
because really, who wants to talk to a person like that?

447
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:25,000
Okay, back down off of the soapbox.

448
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:28,000
And once again, Danny, that wasn't intended for you,

449
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:30,000
But I did hear back from Danny.

450
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:32,000
He said: you know what I think I get it.

451
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:34,000
The badge is really just a beginning.

452
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:35,000
It's a springboard.

453
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:37,000
It's the start of a lifelong process

454
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:39,000
And I've never liked the term eagle doubt.

455
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:42,000
And Danny says I think it diminishes the Scout in the award

456
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:44,000
And maybe they're just kids,

457
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:48,000
Maybe they're just boys trying to become men

458
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:50,000
and they have to figure it out as they go.

459
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:51,000
I think you're right, Dan.

460
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:53,000
I think that's the key right

461
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:56,000
Is understanding that anything that we hand a boy

462
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:59,000
in the way of an award is a beginning.

463
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:01,000
They're not fully cooked yet

464
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:05,000
And once you've had the benefit- as I have- of watching them

465
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:07,000
grow up and become adults,

466
00:21:07,000 --> 00:21:11,000
you get to understand that the influences that they had

467
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:13,000
in Scouting remain important to them

468
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:16,000
and have helped shape them into the people that they become.

469
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:19,000
You may not see it when they're 15 or 16

470
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:20,000
as clearly as you would like,

471
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:23,000
but hey, hang on, talk to them,

472
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:25,000
ask them what they understand

473
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:27,000
and then watch as they become adults.

474
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:30,000
You'll see that understanding demonstrated.

475
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:32,000
Wow, deep subjects, huh?

